WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:07.549
- President Franklin
Delano Roosevelt

2
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.719
is a transformative figure
in American history.

3
00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:12.804
He steers the country
out of the Depression

4
00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:15.307
and away from isolationism.

5
00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:17.100
In the summer of 1944.

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.978
Allied forces are
battling across France.

7
00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:23.482
and heavy bombers are striking
the Japanese home islands.

8
00:00:23.482 --> 00:00:28.237
but forcing the Axis powers
to submit to FDR's demand

9
00:00:28.237 --> 00:00:32.241
unconditional surrender
still requires enormous effort

10
00:00:32.241 --> 00:00:35.160
from America and its Allies.

11
00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:37.996
- All wars change the world.

12
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:39.831
but none of them
changed the world

13
00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:41.291
like the Second World War did.

14
00:00:42.084 --> 00:00:43.627
- Japan's on the march;

15
00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:45.128
Germany's on the march.

16
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.881
No one can imagine
the nightmare

17
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.258
they're about to unleash.

18
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.970
The most destructive
war in human history.

19
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.889
- Suddenly. the world
is turned upside down.

20
00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.683
and all hell is let loose.

21
00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:02.604
- The West is stunned by
the speed of the advance.

22
00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:07.276
- You get the Allies
led by the Big Three:

23
00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:09.695
Roosevelt. Churchill. Stalin.

24
00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:14.992
Men who are dealing with
immensely complicated questions.

25
00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:19.204
- It's the biggest military
operation of human history.

26
00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:20.872
- The Allies have
to come together.

27
00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:23.625
not just militarily.
but industrial scale.

28
00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.877
It's a global perspective.

29
00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:27.546
- They have to fight
in every climate.

30
00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:30.841
from the Arctic to the
jungles of the Pacific.

31
00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:32.759
to the deserts of Africa.

32
00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.803
and the depths of the ocean.

33
00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:40.475
- But there was no
certainty of victory.

34
00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:43.603
It was going to be a
horrific bloodbath.

35
00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.772
- We see humans at
their absolute worst.

36
00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.233
how they treat
other human beings.

37
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:49.860
and we see them at
their absolute best.

38
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.487
willing to give their lives
that others might live.

39
00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:54.614
- World War II was a struggle

40
00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.241
in which there
could be one victor

41
00:01:57.451 --> 00:01:59.202
and one vanquished.

42
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

43
00:02:20.474 --> 00:02:22.434
- Summer. 1944.

44
00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.439
While American forces
combat Germany in Europe

45
00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:29.358
and Japan in the Asia Pacific.

46
00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:32.944
in the United States.
it's an election year.

47
00:02:34.321 --> 00:02:36.365
Even though his
nation is at war.

48
00:02:36.365 --> 00:02:39.618
the Commander In Chief. the
most powerful man in the world.

49
00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.871
must seek the
approval of voters.

50
00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:46.958
- Franklin Roosevelt is
trying to do something

51
00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:48.460
no American has ever done.

52
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.880
which is be elected to a
fourth term as President.

53
00:02:52.964 --> 00:02:55.801
and the country is not ready

54
00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:58.804
to turn Franklin Roosevelt out.

55
00:02:58.804 --> 00:03:02.808
but they're not thrilled
about rehiring him either.

56
00:03:02.808 --> 00:03:04.768
- Roosevelt wasn't
in robust health

57
00:03:04.768 --> 00:03:06.645
when he first ran for
President in 1932.

58
00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:09.398
He was a polio victim
as a young man;

59
00:03:09.398 --> 00:03:11.483
he's in a wheelchair.

60
00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:13.443
Those who've known
him before the war

61
00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:15.112
always come away a bit shocked

62
00:03:15.112 --> 00:03:17.948
at how dramatically
he seemed to age.

63
00:03:17.948 --> 00:03:21.451
- The stress of the war
has ground him down.

64
00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:24.162
so he's got to demonstrate
to the American people

65
00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:26.832
that he is still firmly
in command in this war

66
00:03:26.832 --> 00:03:29.584
and can bring it to
a successful close.

67
00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:39.094
Roosevelt decides to
sail over to Hawaii

68
00:03:39.094 --> 00:03:43.515
to hold a wartime conference
with his top leaders.

69
00:03:44.975 --> 00:03:49.729
By being on a warship at the
site where the war started.

70
00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.273
for the Americans.

71
00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:55.402
he has the ability
to project an image

72
00:03:55.402 --> 00:03:58.280
of continued vitality
and strength.

73
00:04:00.532 --> 00:04:03.493
- The primary purpose
of the Pearl Harbor Conference

74
00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:06.496
is to determine American
strategy against Japan

75
00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:07.706
in the Pacific.

76
00:04:09.124 --> 00:04:10.041
- Roosevelt looks at
Japan

77
00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:11.460
and sees a country infected

78
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.587
with a kind of militarism.

79
00:04:16.423 --> 00:04:18.800
a desire to conquer
its neighbors.

80
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.635
a notion of racial superiority

81
00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:22.846
over all the other
peoples in Asia.

82
00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.768
- To prevent another
Pearl Harbor.

83
00:04:27.768 --> 00:04:29.603
he's going to have to
stick to the policy

84
00:04:29.603 --> 00:04:33.023
he declared in 1943:
unconditional surrender.

85
00:04:34.483 --> 00:04:37.444
- The United States
controls most of the Pacific.

86
00:04:37.444 --> 00:04:38.779
including New Guinea

87
00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:41.072
and the recently
captured Mariana Islands.

88
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.534
The Navy wants to push
on and take Formosa.

89
00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.369
modern-day Taiwan.

90
00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.497
as a prelude to invading Japan.

91
00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.293
General Douglas MacArthur
has other ideas.

92
00:04:54.753 --> 00:04:57.005
- Douglas MacArthur is
one of the most famous

93
00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:59.299
American generals in history.

94
00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:01.510
also one of the
most controversial.

95
00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.012
He's got an ego that
is unbelievable.

96
00:05:05.347 --> 00:05:08.058
In Hawaii. he shows up late.

97
00:05:08.058 --> 00:05:10.811
He keeps the President
waiting on purpose.

98
00:05:11.812 --> 00:05:14.731
- And then rolls in.
in this leather jacket.

99
00:05:14.731 --> 00:05:16.107
decidedly casual.

100
00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.443
in complete contrast
to the Navy.

101
00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:20.862
which is all turned out
in their dress whites.

102
00:05:22.656 --> 00:05:24.449
- MacArthur believes
he's more important

103
00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:26.243
to the country than the
President.

104
00:05:26.243 --> 00:05:28.411
In fact. he believes
he is the country.

105
00:05:28.411 --> 00:05:30.956
that his will and his
desires in some way

106
00:05:30.956 --> 00:05:34.000
embody the will and desire
of the American people.

107
00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:39.089
- MacArthur insists
American forces must return

108
00:05:39.089 --> 00:05:41.216
to the Philippines

109
00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:45.136
for reasons both
strategic and personal.

110
00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:50.642
- Douglas MacArthur
had a long-standing affiliation

111
00:05:50.642 --> 00:05:52.602
with the Philippines.

112
00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:55.480
He had a real affinity
for the Filipino people.

113
00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.440
He had lived there
before the war.

114
00:05:58.316 --> 00:06:01.403
and then in 1941 and 1942.

115
00:06:01.403 --> 00:06:03.905
he was the commander in
chief of that garrison

116
00:06:03.905 --> 00:06:05.323
when it was attacked
by the Japanese

117
00:06:05.323 --> 00:06:07.242
at the very
beginning of the war.

118
00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:13.081
- When Japanese forces
overran the Philippines

119
00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:14.749
in 1942.

120
00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:17.794
Roosevelt ordered
General MacArthur

121
00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:20.672
to evacuate to Australia.

122
00:06:20.672 --> 00:06:22.883
He understood MacArthur's
personal value

123
00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:25.093
to the morale of
the American public.

124
00:06:27.178 --> 00:06:29.723
After months of bitter fighting.

125
00:06:29.723 --> 00:06:33.018
American and Filipino troops
surrendered to the Japanese.

126
00:06:34.019 --> 00:06:35.687
In the cruelest conditions.

127
00:06:35.687 --> 00:06:38.315
they were marched off
the Bataan Peninsula

128
00:06:38.315 --> 00:06:39.608
and into captivity:

129
00:06:41.026 --> 00:06:42.444
the Bataan Death March.

130
00:06:44.696 --> 00:06:46.698
During the Japanese occupation.

131
00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:49.993
hundreds of thousands
of Filipinos are killed.

132
00:06:50.994 --> 00:06:54.247
Women are forced
into sex slavery;

133
00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:58.585
entire villages are
destroyed in mass executions.

134
00:06:58.585 --> 00:06:59.961
- As he is about to leave.

135
00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:02.047
MacArthur makes this
famous pronouncement:

136
00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:05.675
- I shall return.

137
00:07:08.011 --> 00:07:09.471
- It's not "we shall return";

138
00:07:09.471 --> 00:07:11.306
it's "I shall return."

139
00:07:11.306 --> 00:07:14.392
- This is a vow that becomes
really a rallying cry

140
00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:15.727
for Americans.

141
00:07:15.727 --> 00:07:17.896
not only in the Philippines
who are captured.

142
00:07:17.896 --> 00:07:19.606
but also for
Americans back home.

143
00:07:21.316 --> 00:07:22.734
- At Pearl Harbor.

144
00:07:22.734 --> 00:07:24.819
MacArthur presses the
President to strike Japan

145
00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:27.280
through the Philippines
instead of Formosa.

146
00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:32.911
- The problem with
the Philippines

147
00:07:32.911 --> 00:07:34.704
is it's not just an island;

148
00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:36.206
it's an archipelago.

149
00:07:36.414 --> 00:07:39.042
And there's a very large
Japanese army on these islands.

150
00:07:39.042 --> 00:07:41.169
hundreds of thousands of troops

151
00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:44.881
that could lead to protracted
fighting with the Japanese.

152
00:07:47.425 --> 00:07:50.762
- But MacArthur has some
arguments on his side as well.

153
00:07:50.762 --> 00:07:53.306
The Philippines are an
American possession.

154
00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:55.016
They're a commonwealth.

155
00:07:55.016 --> 00:07:58.937
To simply bypass the
Philippines and go to Taiwan

156
00:07:58.937 --> 00:08:03.066
will make America look like
a very inconstant partner.

157
00:08:03.066 --> 00:08:05.402
not to mention
abandoning the survivors

158
00:08:05.402 --> 00:08:07.278
of the Bataan Death March.

159
00:08:08.446 --> 00:08:10.240
- Roosevelt
leaves the conference

160
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.783
without making a decision.

161
00:08:12.575 --> 00:08:14.285
A week later. though.

162
00:08:14.285 --> 00:08:18.039
he sends a letter to General
MacArthur agreeing to his plan.

163
00:08:27.048 --> 00:08:29.008
On October 20th.

164
00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:33.304
over a hundred
thousand U.S. troops

165
00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:37.767
backed by the Seventh and Third
U.S. Fleets land on Leyte.

166
00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:39.769
a central island in
the Philippines

167
00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:42.063
with a protected gulf.

168
00:08:42.813 --> 00:08:46.151
MacArthur's campaign to
retake the country has begun.

169
00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:54.617
- He had the Army Signal
Corps of Engineers go ahead

170
00:08:54.617 --> 00:08:56.161
on the beach ahead of him.

171
00:08:58.163 --> 00:09:00.165
so by the time he got there.

172
00:09:00.165 --> 00:09:02.167
he gives his radio address.

173
00:09:02.917 --> 00:09:06.755
- People of the
Philippines. I have returned.

174
00:09:11.092 --> 00:09:14.429
- The fact that MacArthur
is able to return

175
00:09:14.429 --> 00:09:19.059
to fulfill that promise
is a morale boost.

176
00:09:19.059 --> 00:09:20.852
But the theater of
MacArthur saying.

177
00:09:20.852 --> 00:09:22.353
"I have returned."

178
00:09:22.353 --> 00:09:26.691
does not translate into
straightforward Allied victory.

179
00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:31.362
- The Japanese know

180
00:09:31.362 --> 00:09:35.325
that General MacArthur
has promised to return.

181
00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:41.372
and so they anticipated
the next decisive battle

182
00:09:42.082 --> 00:09:44.250
may be over the Philippines.

183
00:09:45.418 --> 00:09:47.587
- The Philippines
sit between Japan

184
00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:49.464
and the Dutch East Indies.

185
00:09:49.464 --> 00:09:52.926
Japan's primary source of oil.

186
00:09:52.926 --> 00:09:55.470
- So if the
Philippines get captured.

187
00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.472
it will cut off
the supply lines.

188
00:09:57.472 --> 00:09:59.724
and the military
runs out of oil.

189
00:10:01.684 --> 00:10:03.561
- The Japanese
already have a plan

190
00:10:03.561 --> 00:10:05.480
to defend the Philippines:

191
00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:08.441
Operation Sho-Go. or victory.

192
00:10:09.567 --> 00:10:11.111
They will send
all their warships

193
00:10:11.111 --> 00:10:14.072
to destroy the U.S.
fleets in Leyte Gulf.

194
00:10:14.072 --> 00:10:17.992
then trap General MacArthur's
troops on the island.

195
00:10:17.992 --> 00:10:19.828
- The Japanese
position is

196
00:10:19.828 --> 00:10:24.082
it's absolutely imperative to
carry out this operation now.

197
00:10:24.082 --> 00:10:26.376
We've got oil.
we've got warships.

198
00:10:26.376 --> 00:10:28.336
We better use them or lose them.

199
00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.050
- This is almost like one
last banzai charge at sea

200
00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.594
that can have a material effect

201
00:10:36.594 --> 00:10:37.804
on the outcome of the war.

202
00:10:38.847 --> 00:10:40.431
That's the kind of spirit

203
00:10:40.431 --> 00:10:42.767
in which the Japanese are
heading into this battle.

204
00:10:43.601 --> 00:10:45.228
- The Allies have
promised the Japanese

205
00:10:45.228 --> 00:10:46.771
that the war's gonna go on

206
00:10:46.896 --> 00:10:49.440
until unconditional surrender
of the Japanese empire.

207
00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:51.651
But deep down inside.

208
00:10:51.651 --> 00:10:53.945
I'm not sure the
Japanese believe that.

209
00:10:53.945 --> 00:10:56.865
They still think they can
inflict enough casualties

210
00:10:56.865 --> 00:10:59.075
on the United States
that eventually.

211
00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:01.536
despite all the talk of
unconditional surrender.

212
00:11:01.536 --> 00:11:04.080
the Americans will
be willing to talk.

213
00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:10.295
- As MacArthur and
his troops land on Leyte.

214
00:11:10.295 --> 00:11:13.756
the Japanese fleets move
towards the Philippines.

215
00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.134
- At this point in
the war.

216
00:11:16.134 --> 00:11:19.554
the Japanese have suffered
terrible losses in the Pacific

217
00:11:19.554 --> 00:11:21.973
from Midway to Guadalcanal.

218
00:11:21.973 --> 00:11:24.642
but they still have a
tremendously powerful fleet.

219
00:11:25.643 --> 00:11:26.936
They have these
super battleships.

220
00:11:26.936 --> 00:11:29.314
the Musashi and the Yamato.

221
00:11:30.607 --> 00:11:33.401
- These are the biggest
battleships ever built.

222
00:11:33.401 --> 00:11:36.654
and this has always been
the U.S. Navy's fear.

223
00:11:36.654 --> 00:11:41.284
If these massive Yamato-class
battleships get loose

224
00:11:41.284 --> 00:11:43.995
in amongst MacArthur's
troop ships.

225
00:11:43.995 --> 00:11:46.247
the invasion support vessels.

226
00:11:46.247 --> 00:11:48.166
the devastation
could be immense.

227
00:11:50.251 --> 00:11:52.670
- Two Japanese fleets.

228
00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:54.881
a central fleet and
a southern fleet.

229
00:11:54.881 --> 00:11:57.258
will converge on Leyte Gulf.

230
00:11:57.258 --> 00:12:00.970
where U.S. naval forces are
protecting MacArthur's landing.

231
00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.348
Success depends on a third fleet

232
00:12:03.348 --> 00:12:05.683
moving towards the
gulf from the north:

233
00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:08.561
a decoy fleet.

234
00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:09.812
- There's another
aspect.

235
00:12:09.938 --> 00:12:11.856
There's essentially
a carrier task force

236
00:12:11.856 --> 00:12:15.109
that sails towards Leyte
Gulf from the north.

237
00:12:15.109 --> 00:12:17.237
It has four aircraft
carriers in it.

238
00:12:17.237 --> 00:12:19.906
It's designed to try to
lure American ships away

239
00:12:19.906 --> 00:12:21.241
from protecting MacArthur.

240
00:12:22.492 --> 00:12:25.245
- And this is the brilliance
of the Japanese move.

241
00:12:25.245 --> 00:12:27.872
They decide to use
the carriers as bait.

242
00:12:29.082 --> 00:12:32.043
The entire Japanese strategy
is to trap MacArthur.

243
00:12:32.043 --> 00:12:35.004
trap this famous general
himself. potentially.

244
00:12:35.004 --> 00:12:37.465
- They're gonna bring
these two heavily armed

245
00:12:37.465 --> 00:12:41.219
and armored pincers against
the American landing force

246
00:12:41.219 --> 00:12:43.596
while it is still
unloading cargo

247
00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:46.641
and supplies and troops at Leyte

248
00:12:46.641 --> 00:12:47.642
and annihilate it.

249
00:12:55.358 --> 00:12:58.611
- General MacArthur
has landed 130.000 troops

250
00:12:58.611 --> 00:13:00.113
on Leyte.

251
00:13:00.113 --> 00:13:02.198
the first step in the
American liberation

252
00:13:02.198 --> 00:13:03.449
of the Philippines.

253
00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:07.996
But as U.S. troops establish
a secure beachhead.

254
00:13:07.996 --> 00:13:12.417
the Japanese Navy
enacts Operation Sho-Go.

255
00:13:12.417 --> 00:13:14.043
- The Japanese
have to figure out

256
00:13:14.043 --> 00:13:16.879
how to sneak their forces
close to the landing beaches

257
00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:18.214
without being discovered.

258
00:13:18.214 --> 00:13:21.050
because if they come
under heavy air attack.

259
00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:22.552
they're in real trouble.

260
00:13:22.552 --> 00:13:26.222
So they plan to lure most
of the American forces

261
00:13:26.222 --> 00:13:27.348
out of the fight.

262
00:13:28.641 --> 00:13:29.934
- There are
two American fleets

263
00:13:29.934 --> 00:13:32.228
supporting MacArthur's forces:

264
00:13:32.228 --> 00:13:35.523
the U.S. Third Fleet led
by Admiral Bull Halsey

265
00:13:36.733 --> 00:13:39.652
and the Seventh led by
Admiral Thomas Kinkaid.

266
00:13:40.778 --> 00:13:43.531
As the Japanese cross
the Sibuyan Sea.

267
00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:47.702
they're spotted by one
of Halsey's scout planes.

268
00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.960
- The Americans
attacked from the air.

269
00:13:57.336 --> 00:14:00.590
and they better focus on
the largest ships they see.

270
00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.426
which was the super
battleships Yamato and Musashi.

271
00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:07.388
- Musashi is an
absolute giant.

272
00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:10.058
She has 18.1-inch naval guns.

273
00:14:10.058 --> 00:14:12.226
the largest naval
guns ever mounted.

274
00:14:12.977 --> 00:14:14.562
The Japanese have even developed

275
00:14:14.562 --> 00:14:17.982
a special anti-aircraft
shell for them as well.

276
00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:22.111
which explodes in
this enormous cloud.

277
00:14:22.111 --> 00:14:24.697
and it's supposed to
destroy any American planes

278
00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:26.240
that are flying through it.

279
00:14:30.453 --> 00:14:31.829
But the attacks that are

280
00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:33.915
launched by
these American planes

281
00:14:33.915 --> 00:14:35.833
gradually start whittling away

282
00:14:35.833 --> 00:14:37.418
at her defensive capabilities.

283
00:14:39.587 --> 00:14:41.798
- Torpedo after
torpedo

284
00:14:41.798 --> 00:14:43.925
streaks towards the Musashi.

285
00:14:43.925 --> 00:14:47.095
hitting and exploding
below the waterline.

286
00:14:47.095 --> 00:14:50.223
- Seventeen to 19
torpedoes.

287
00:14:50.223 --> 00:14:52.100
more than a dozen bombs.

288
00:14:54.143 --> 00:14:56.354
finally get her to go down.

289
00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:04.070
- The Musashi sinks along
with a thousand of her crew.

290
00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.280
The Japanese central
fleet retreats.

291
00:15:07.615 --> 00:15:10.034
That night. Kinkaid's fleet.

292
00:15:10.034 --> 00:15:11.577
including battleships

293
00:15:11.577 --> 00:15:14.330
that had been damaged
at Pearl Harbor.

294
00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.833
decimate the Japanese
southern fleet.

295
00:15:18.376 --> 00:15:21.337
But Halsey again spots
Japanese warships

296
00:15:21.337 --> 00:15:23.589
north of Leyte Gulf.

297
00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:26.175
including four
aircraft carriers:

298
00:15:26.175 --> 00:15:28.052
the Japanese decoy fleet.

299
00:15:29.428 --> 00:15:30.721
- In Halsey's mind.

300
00:15:30.721 --> 00:15:32.890
as a carrier commander himself.

301
00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:36.936
any enemy carrier fleet
represents the most dire threat

302
00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:38.396
to his mission.

303
00:15:38.396 --> 00:15:41.440
which is protecting
the invasion forces.

304
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.693
So Halsey takes his entire fleet

305
00:15:43.693 --> 00:15:46.112
and goes charging off after it.

306
00:15:46.112 --> 00:15:48.489
- And so Halsey's
played into the Japanese hands

307
00:15:48.489 --> 00:15:50.491
exactly as they had hoped.

308
00:15:52.535 --> 00:15:55.746
- Halsey's taken all
the major firepower

309
00:15:55.746 --> 00:16:00.793
north with him. and that puts
MacArthur's invasion at risk.

310
00:16:05.131 --> 00:16:07.258
- Halsey moves north.

311
00:16:07.258 --> 00:16:09.385
but the Japanese central fleet

312
00:16:09.385 --> 00:16:13.556
under Admiral Takeo Kurita
returns to Leyte Gulf.

313
00:16:15.766 --> 00:16:18.227
From the battleship Yamato.

314
00:16:18.227 --> 00:16:20.813
Kurita sees that only a
portion of the Seventh Fleet

315
00:16:20.813 --> 00:16:23.524
is left to protect
MacArthur's invasion.

316
00:16:23.524 --> 00:16:28.321
- For Kurita. this is
a heaven-sent opportunity.

317
00:16:28.321 --> 00:16:30.198
The dawn breaks.

318
00:16:32.158 --> 00:16:35.077
and now there is
an American fleet

319
00:16:35.077 --> 00:16:37.955
sitting directly under his guns.

320
00:16:37.955 --> 00:16:39.749
All he has to do is sink them.

321
00:16:41.083 --> 00:16:42.585
- The American
vessels

322
00:16:42.585 --> 00:16:46.339
that are still around Leyte
primarily provide support

323
00:16:46.339 --> 00:16:48.633
for the landing
forces going ashore.

324
00:16:48.633 --> 00:16:50.593
So there's only a token force

325
00:16:50.593 --> 00:16:53.471
of light escort
carriers and destroyers.

326
00:16:55.306 --> 00:16:59.060
- Escort carriers are
small. extremely vulnerable.

327
00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:03.147
They can carry 25
to 30 aircraft max.

328
00:17:03.147 --> 00:17:05.148
They're designed
for ground support

329
00:17:05.148 --> 00:17:06.983
for the troops ashore.

330
00:17:06.983 --> 00:17:08.319
That's their role.

331
00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:11.489
It is not to get themselves
into standup fights

332
00:17:11.489 --> 00:17:13.950
against large enemy warships.

333
00:17:15.701 --> 00:17:17.411
- The escort carriers
launch their planes

334
00:17:17.411 --> 00:17:19.497
against Kurita's
much larger fleet.

335
00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:22.250
- And then these
escort carriers.

336
00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.252
mostly what they do is
turn around and run.

337
00:17:24.252 --> 00:17:27.338
because they are not designed
to fight a threat like this.

338
00:17:29.549 --> 00:17:32.510
- The only force that
can match the Japanese.

339
00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:34.512
Admiral Halsey's Third Fleet.

340
00:17:34.512 --> 00:17:37.598
remains a hundred
miles to the north.

341
00:17:37.598 --> 00:17:40.768
His commander in chief.
Admiral Chester Nimitz.

342
00:17:40.768 --> 00:17:43.104
requests his location.

343
00:17:43.104 --> 00:17:46.274
- Halsey receives a
message from Nimitz:

344
00:17:46.274 --> 00:17:50.361
"Where. repeat.
where. is Task Force 34

345
00:17:50.361 --> 00:17:52.280
The world wonders."

346
00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:54.615
Traditionally. naval
messages begin and end

347
00:17:54.615 --> 00:17:56.117
with sort of a nonsense phrase

348
00:17:56.117 --> 00:17:58.911
to confuse code breakers.

349
00:17:58.911 --> 00:18:01.080
The nonsense phrase they
used in this message. though.

350
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.708
is "the world wonders."

351
00:18:03.708 --> 00:18:05.418
- And the "world
wonders" line

352
00:18:05.418 --> 00:18:09.297
should have been removed by his
cipher officer. but wasn't.

353
00:18:10.172 --> 00:18:11.966
- I've always
wondered if Admiral Nimitz

354
00:18:11.966 --> 00:18:14.468
was also sticking a little
barb in Halsey as to.

355
00:18:14.468 --> 00:18:15.970
"What are you doing up there?"

356
00:18:17.471 --> 00:18:19.682
- And the result is
that Halsey

357
00:18:19.682 --> 00:18:21.267
just goes into conniptions.

358
00:18:21.267 --> 00:18:23.227
throws his cap on the floor.

359
00:18:23.227 --> 00:18:25.271
swearing up and down.

360
00:18:25.271 --> 00:18:28.190
His chief of staff
has to tell him

361
00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:30.401
to get a grip on this situation.

362
00:18:31.819 --> 00:18:34.280
- Halsey
realizes the danger.

363
00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:38.034
He sends his fastest
battleships back to Leyte Gulf.

364
00:18:38.034 --> 00:18:41.162
then continues after
the Japanese carriers.

365
00:18:42.163 --> 00:18:43.706
- Now. what the
Americans don't know

366
00:18:43.706 --> 00:18:46.042
is that the Japanese carriers
lying to Halsey's north

367
00:18:46.042 --> 00:18:47.543
don't have many planes on them.

368
00:18:47.543 --> 00:18:50.129
In fact. most of the planes have
been offloaded already

369
00:18:50.129 --> 00:18:52.298
and flown to the island of Luzon

370
00:18:52.298 --> 00:18:53.883
for what is about to come.

371
00:18:53.883 --> 00:18:55.843
because a new and
terrifying weapon

372
00:18:55.843 --> 00:18:57.637
emerges from this battle:

373
00:19:00.723 --> 00:19:01.766
the kamikaze.

374
00:19:13.277 --> 00:19:15.279
- At an airfield on Luzon.

375
00:19:16.238 --> 00:19:20.117
Japanese pilots are assembled
for a unique mission.

376
00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.537
- Japanese pilots at
the beginning of the war

377
00:19:23.537 --> 00:19:25.539
were the cream of the crop.

378
00:19:25.539 --> 00:19:29.335
elite. hand-selected.
and carefully trained.

379
00:19:29.919 --> 00:19:31.921
But by this point in the war.
Japan

380
00:19:31.921 --> 00:19:35.049
just doesn't have the time or
the resources

381
00:19:35.049 --> 00:19:36.717
to train its pilots.

382
00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.930
- The Japanese military
leadership

383
00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:43.516
has to make a tough decision

384
00:19:43.516 --> 00:19:46.727
to make a cost-efficient way

385
00:19:46.727 --> 00:19:49.522
of destroying
American enemy ships.

386
00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:56.237
And one way to do it is to
undertake one-way flight

387
00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:02.201
that will end with
crashing a plane.

388
00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:07.039
- This new
unit of 24 pilots.

389
00:20:07.039 --> 00:20:11.043
led by 23-year-old
Lieutenant Yukio Seki.

390
00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:15.631
is Japan's first organized
special attack unit.

391
00:20:15.631 --> 00:20:18.217
They will soon become
known as kamikaze:

392
00:20:19.427 --> 00:20:20.886
the divine wind.

393
00:20:23.305 --> 00:20:25.683
- So the kamikaze in
some ways represents

394
00:20:25.683 --> 00:20:29.812
a perversion of traditional
Japanese bushido doctrine.

395
00:20:29.812 --> 00:20:31.105
the samurai code.

396
00:20:31.105 --> 00:20:33.983
this idea of sacrificing
one's life for Japan

397
00:20:33.983 --> 00:20:35.401
and for the emperor.

398
00:20:36.736 --> 00:20:37.737
- It's hard to
generalize

399
00:20:37.737 --> 00:20:39.196
about their motivations.

400
00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:41.323
You find some of them saying.

401
00:20:41.323 --> 00:20:43.617
"I'm doing this
for the emperor."

402
00:20:43.617 --> 00:20:46.162
Other pilots talk
about their village.

403
00:20:46.162 --> 00:20:47.413
they talk about families.

404
00:20:47.413 --> 00:20:49.623
and they say. "I'm
doing this for you."

405
00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:52.710
For the Japanese.
what they're doing

406
00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:54.920
was not regarded as suicide.

407
00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.630
It's a death in combat.

408
00:20:57.298 --> 00:20:59.049
So it's this sense that

409
00:20:59.049 --> 00:21:01.677
these young men are becoming
something really special.

410
00:21:01.677 --> 00:21:04.430
that almost to give this
level of self-sacrifice

411
00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:06.390
makes you a samurai.

412
00:21:11.687 --> 00:21:13.355
- In Leyte Gulf.

413
00:21:13.355 --> 00:21:16.275
the American escort carriers
of the U.S. Seventh Fleet

414
00:21:16.275 --> 00:21:19.737
confront a new and lethal
threat from the air.

415
00:21:30.915 --> 00:21:35.002
- Stopping a kamikaze
plane is almost impossible.

416
00:21:37.963 --> 00:21:40.841
The American carriers
can only do two things.

417
00:21:40.841 --> 00:21:43.344
One is maneuver away.

418
00:21:45.179 --> 00:21:47.973
and the second is put up
a huge curtain of flak.

419
00:21:50.476 --> 00:21:51.977
But oftentimes

420
00:21:51.977 --> 00:21:54.271
the kamikazes can break
through that flak curtain.

421
00:21:55.648 --> 00:21:58.609
- You could fire every
anti-aircraft gun in the world.

422
00:21:58.609 --> 00:22:01.362
but unless you get
something like a direct hit

423
00:22:01.362 --> 00:22:02.696
that kills the pilot.

424
00:22:04.365 --> 00:22:06.200
that plane's gonna
hit your ship.

425
00:22:10.037 --> 00:22:12.164
- There's a
psychological effect as well.

426
00:22:13.207 --> 00:22:15.376
Japanese kamikaze are
giving up their lives

427
00:22:15.376 --> 00:22:16.961
in the most violent
ways possible

428
00:22:16.961 --> 00:22:18.712
simply to kill Americans.

429
00:22:19.839 --> 00:22:20.923
This is something
that is difficult

430
00:22:20.923 --> 00:22:22.508
for Americans to understand.

431
00:22:22.508 --> 00:22:23.884
that this is a human being

432
00:22:23.884 --> 00:22:26.720
who has chosen to do
this to you.

433
00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:28.973
How do you fight against
an enemy like this?

434
00:22:30.057 --> 00:22:31.976
- Lieutenant
Seki crashes his plane

435
00:22:31.976 --> 00:22:35.437
into the flight deck of an
American escort carrier.

436
00:22:35.437 --> 00:22:37.106
the USS St. Lo.

437
00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:41.735
It's the first ship to be
sunk by kamikaze attack.

438
00:22:43.153 --> 00:22:46.407
Six other vessels are struck
and barely stay afloat.

439
00:22:49.952 --> 00:22:51.537
But Admiral Kurita's
central fleet

440
00:22:51.537 --> 00:22:53.998
also faces an aerial attack.

441
00:22:54.957 --> 00:22:57.418
- Despite the pounding
the Americans have taken.

442
00:22:57.418 --> 00:22:59.003
it is actually Kurita

443
00:22:59.003 --> 00:23:01.255
who is under a major
disadvantage here.

444
00:23:01.255 --> 00:23:04.258
He's up against 350
American planes.

445
00:23:05.843 --> 00:23:08.929
and because Kurita is
being constantly attacked

446
00:23:08.929 --> 00:23:10.347
by American aircraft.

447
00:23:11.265 --> 00:23:13.601
he becomes increasingly paranoid

448
00:23:13.601 --> 00:23:16.604
that he is now surrounded
by American carriers.

449
00:23:17.897 --> 00:23:19.440
- So after these
moments of tension

450
00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:21.734
where Kurita could have
potentially wreaked havoc

451
00:23:21.734 --> 00:23:23.777
on the American
fleet in Leyte Gulf.

452
00:23:23.777 --> 00:23:26.030
he actually withdraws.

453
00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:27.323
- Kurita retreats ahead

454
00:23:27.323 --> 00:23:30.492
of Admiral Halsey's
returning warships.

455
00:23:30.492 --> 00:23:33.954
but the Japanese have lost
a third of their navy.

456
00:23:35.372 --> 00:23:37.875
- It's the largest
naval battle in human history.

457
00:23:37.875 --> 00:23:40.210
More than 200.000
combatants involved.

458
00:23:41.211 --> 00:23:43.130
and the American
victory really knocks

459
00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:46.133
the Japanese Imperial Navy
out of the war in the Pacific.

460
00:23:46.133 --> 00:23:47.384
- But without
a doubt.

461
00:23:47.384 --> 00:23:49.053
the Japanese are
not gonna surrender.

462
00:23:49.053 --> 00:23:51.972
This perversion of
the bushido code.

463
00:23:51.972 --> 00:23:54.767
it has manifested itself on
islands throughout this war.

464
00:23:54.767 --> 00:23:58.312
and now it's manifesting itself
in the sea and in the air.

465
00:23:58.312 --> 00:24:00.189
So it's starting to get
in the mind of the Allies

466
00:24:00.189 --> 00:24:02.316
about how costly it will be

467
00:24:02.316 --> 00:24:05.277
to achieve that unconditional
surrender of Japan.

468
00:24:07.488 --> 00:24:11.200
- Over two months of
fighting in dense jungle terrain

469
00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:13.994
against fierce
Japanese resistance.

470
00:24:13.994 --> 00:24:16.914
General MacArthur's
troops retake Leyte.

471
00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.793
Luzon is next.

472
00:24:20.793 --> 00:24:22.544
- Luzon in particular

473
00:24:22.544 --> 00:24:24.672
is gonna be a very tough nut to
crack.

474
00:24:24.672 --> 00:24:28.133
because not only is it a large
island with mountain chains

475
00:24:28.133 --> 00:24:29.843
the Japanese can fortify.

476
00:24:29.843 --> 00:24:32.721
but there's also this
enormous capital of Manila

477
00:24:32.721 --> 00:24:34.306
with about a million people

478
00:24:34.306 --> 00:24:36.016
sitting in the
middle of the island.

479
00:24:36.016 --> 00:24:38.185
And if the American
forces get involved

480
00:24:38.185 --> 00:24:39.937
in urban combat there.

481
00:24:39.937 --> 00:24:42.481
the casualties are likely
to be very. very heavy.

482
00:24:47.987 --> 00:24:50.656
- Twelve days after
the victory at Leyte Gulf.

483
00:24:50.656 --> 00:24:52.908
it's election night
in the United States.

484
00:24:52.908 --> 00:24:54.284
- At Hyde Park.

485
00:24:54.284 --> 00:24:56.245
a victorious President
greets townspeople

486
00:24:56.245 --> 00:24:58.038
on election night.

487
00:24:58.038 --> 00:24:59.373
- Franklin
D. Roosevelt wins

488
00:24:59.373 --> 00:25:01.208
an unprecedented fourth term.

489
00:25:02.626 --> 00:25:06.046
The unconditional surrender
of Japan and Germany

490
00:25:06.046 --> 00:25:08.674
remains his highest priority.

491
00:25:16.515 --> 00:25:18.809
On January 9th. 1945.

492
00:25:18.809 --> 00:25:22.646
MacArthur's troops land on
Luzon in the Philippines.

493
00:25:22.646 --> 00:25:26.025
Quickly. special operation
forces trek inland

494
00:25:26.025 --> 00:25:28.610
to liberate Japanese POW camps.

495
00:25:34.074 --> 00:25:36.368
- At the end of two
years of captivity

496
00:25:36.368 --> 00:25:39.204
in Japanese hands. these men are
in terrible condition.

497
00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.916
A third of them
have already died.

498
00:25:42.916 --> 00:25:46.962
There are also a number of
American civilian detainees

499
00:25:46.962 --> 00:25:49.923
that are in camps
that are near Manila.

500
00:25:51.258 --> 00:25:53.594
so there's a ticking clock here

501
00:25:53.594 --> 00:25:55.179
as far as MacArthur
is concerned.

502
00:25:55.179 --> 00:25:59.975
He needs to liberate these
camps as rapidly as possible.

503
00:26:02.561 --> 00:26:03.854
- In early February.

504
00:26:03.979 --> 00:26:07.107
American forces surround
the capital of Manila.

505
00:26:07.107 --> 00:26:09.193
Most Japanese troops
have retreated north

506
00:26:09.193 --> 00:26:12.112
to create an extensive
defensive line.

507
00:26:12.112 --> 00:26:17.159
but one group stays with orders
to fight to the last man.

508
00:26:19.828 --> 00:26:22.956
- The real problem of
urban warfare in the modern era

509
00:26:22.956 --> 00:26:26.168
is that the city is
a gigantic sponge.

510
00:26:26.168 --> 00:26:29.963
You can have a million-man army.

511
00:26:29.963 --> 00:26:32.883
and suddenly it's split
up all over the place.

512
00:26:34.176 --> 00:26:36.053
- So the Americans
find themselves having to fight

513
00:26:36.053 --> 00:26:38.764
street by street.
building by building.

514
00:26:40.015 --> 00:26:43.185
and it really turns
Manila into the Stalingrad

515
00:26:43.185 --> 00:26:44.520
of the Pacific.

516
00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.070
- MacArthur's
men are supported

517
00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:56.907
by Filipino resistance
fighters who provide intel.

518
00:26:56.907 --> 00:26:59.118
disrupt Japanese supply lines.

519
00:26:59.118 --> 00:27:02.162
and help secure key
routes into the city.

520
00:27:02.162 --> 00:27:04.790
- There have been
active groups of

521
00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:08.585
Filipino guerrillas in existence
since 1942.

522
00:27:11.046 --> 00:27:12.965
and those guerrillas
have been carrying on

523
00:27:12.965 --> 00:27:15.384
a very successful
campaign of resistance

524
00:27:15.384 --> 00:27:17.970
against the Japanese occupiers.

525
00:27:22.391 --> 00:27:25.936
- The Japanese response.
like the Nazis in Europe.

526
00:27:25.936 --> 00:27:27.938
was to use guerrilla activity

527
00:27:27.938 --> 00:27:30.691
as an excuse to
murder civilians.

528
00:27:33.944 --> 00:27:36.905
- So whether it's a
school. a hospital. homes.

529
00:27:36.905 --> 00:27:39.658
Filipino people are
being bayoneted.

530
00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:43.829
they're being shot. beheaded.
sometimes burned alive.

531
00:27:43.829 --> 00:27:45.372
corpses left to rot.

532
00:27:47.207 --> 00:27:49.209
There's a sense
of scorched earth.

533
00:27:49.209 --> 00:27:52.129
a sense that we will leave
nothing for the Americans.

534
00:27:53.672 --> 00:27:55.340
- The Japanese will
ultimately take the lives

535
00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:57.885
of a hundred thousand
Filipino civilians.

536
00:27:59.011 --> 00:28:00.679
The Battle of Manila.
in a lot of ways.

537
00:28:00.679 --> 00:28:02.306
is a harbinger of
what's to come.

538
00:28:04.975 --> 00:28:09.563
- Roosevelt and his
top military advisors are aware

539
00:28:09.563 --> 00:28:12.733
of the increasingly
dire implications

540
00:28:12.733 --> 00:28:15.402
of a longer war with Japan.

541
00:28:15.402 --> 00:28:17.905
but the American
people are very focused

542
00:28:17.905 --> 00:28:19.948
on what's going on in Europe.

543
00:28:19.948 --> 00:28:23.535
and they think the war
is now winding down.

544
00:28:24.745 --> 00:28:27.331
- President Roosevelt's
just been reelected.

545
00:28:27.331 --> 00:28:28.999
He's looking at the horrors.

546
00:28:28.999 --> 00:28:31.668
the brutality of the
fighting in the Philippines.

547
00:28:31.668 --> 00:28:33.295
and he's been
peppered with letters

548
00:28:33.295 --> 00:28:34.630
from the moms of America saying.

549
00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:35.631
"Where is my boy?

550
00:28:35.631 --> 00:28:37.132
What is he doing?

551
00:28:37.132 --> 00:28:39.843
What God-forsaken island is
he fighting on this time?"

552
00:28:39.843 --> 00:28:42.346
He needs to find a way
to strengthen the will

553
00:28:42.346 --> 00:28:44.348
and resolve of the
American people.

554
00:28:44.348 --> 00:28:46.808
because he knows things are
going to get a lot worse

555
00:28:46.808 --> 00:28:48.810
before they get better.

556
00:28:54.816 --> 00:28:57.361
- Roosevelt meets
with British Prime Minister

557
00:28:57.361 --> 00:28:59.446
Winston Churchill in Malta

558
00:28:59.446 --> 00:29:02.574
to discuss the strategy for
ending the war in Europe

559
00:29:02.574 --> 00:29:04.493
and in the Pacific.

560
00:29:04.493 --> 00:29:06.119
- The question is.

561
00:29:06.119 --> 00:29:08.246
if we have an enemy willing
to sacrifice young men

562
00:29:08.246 --> 00:29:10.624
in kamikaze attacks.

563
00:29:10.624 --> 00:29:14.211
an enemy that seems willing
to fight on beyond reason.

564
00:29:14.211 --> 00:29:16.797
how are we going to
bring this to an end?

565
00:29:16.797 --> 00:29:19.049
- Roosevelt's
advisors are arguing

566
00:29:19.049 --> 00:29:21.468
that the Japanese
will not give up.

567
00:29:21.468 --> 00:29:23.220
This war will not end

568
00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:26.139
until there is
actually an invasion

569
00:29:26.139 --> 00:29:27.474
of the home islands.

570
00:29:29.059 --> 00:29:31.353
- Churchill and
Roosevelt are in Malta

571
00:29:31.353 --> 00:29:34.648
on the way to Yalta
early in 1945.

572
00:29:34.648 --> 00:29:38.819
and this is where Operation
Downfall first crystallizes:

573
00:29:38.819 --> 00:29:40.153
the invasion of Japan.

574
00:29:42.447 --> 00:29:45.575
The sense is that after the
war in Europe would be over.

575
00:29:45.575 --> 00:29:47.536
and all of the
resources. all the men.

576
00:29:47.536 --> 00:29:49.329
all the equipment
that they could muster

577
00:29:49.329 --> 00:29:52.082
would be transferred
into the Far East theater

578
00:29:52.082 --> 00:29:55.043
for a massive.
massive operation.

579
00:29:55.043 --> 00:29:57.838
the kind of which history
has never seen before.

580
00:29:57.838 --> 00:29:59.172
- This would occur

581
00:29:59.172 --> 00:30:01.842
after a protracted
strategic bombing campaign.

582
00:30:01.842 --> 00:30:03.510
which as of early 1945

583
00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.804
is beginning to take
more and more effect.

584
00:30:07.889 --> 00:30:10.934
- At first. the Marines
are ordered to take Iwo Jima.

585
00:30:14.938 --> 00:30:17.566
- Japan has an
airfield on Iwo Jima.

586
00:30:17.566 --> 00:30:20.068
and as the B-29s start operating

587
00:30:20.068 --> 00:30:22.487
out of Saipan and the Marianas.

588
00:30:22.487 --> 00:30:26.033
they're using it to
spot the B-29 formations

589
00:30:26.033 --> 00:30:27.325
on their radar scopes.

590
00:30:29.202 --> 00:30:32.122
- Iwo Jima becomes a
logical place to. first of all.

591
00:30:32.122 --> 00:30:34.791
remove something from
the Japanese control.

592
00:30:34.791 --> 00:30:37.544
Second. don't allow them to
have any Japanese fighter

593
00:30:37.544 --> 00:30:40.505
on there or early
warning systems.

594
00:30:40.505 --> 00:30:42.674
And third. for all those B -29s

595
00:30:42.674 --> 00:30:46.219
that are making those long
flights over 1.200 miles.

596
00:30:46.219 --> 00:30:48.555
it creates a place to land
if they have engine trouble.

597
00:30:48.555 --> 00:30:50.390
if they run into
any difficulties.

598
00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.601
- So Iwo Jima has gotta go.

599
00:30:53.935 --> 00:30:56.521
That needs to become
an American airfield.

600
00:30:57.773 --> 00:31:00.233
- Now. American
intelligence tells the Marines

601
00:31:00.233 --> 00:31:02.944
that there are about
13.000 Japanese defenders.

602
00:31:04.529 --> 00:31:07.115
but there are actually 23.000.

603
00:31:08.366 --> 00:31:11.912
nearly double what the
intelligence estimates listed.

604
00:31:13.163 --> 00:31:16.083
As a result. you have an
American invasion force

605
00:31:16.083 --> 00:31:19.044
that's going to be meeting
a much tougher defense

606
00:31:19.044 --> 00:31:20.670
than it expected.

607
00:31:33.809 --> 00:31:37.270
- Japanese troops on
Iwo Jima have been preparing

608
00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:40.440
for an American attack
for over three months.

609
00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:44.903
- The Japanese strategy
is to make the battle last

610
00:31:44.903 --> 00:31:46.905
as long as possible.

611
00:31:46.905 --> 00:31:49.699
Each day that that battle lasts

612
00:31:49.699 --> 00:31:51.743
is a day where the people
at the home islands

613
00:31:51.743 --> 00:31:54.204
are spared of an
American invasion.

614
00:31:55.455 --> 00:31:58.041
- So the commander
of the Japanese on Iwo Jima.

615
00:31:58.041 --> 00:31:59.960
Kuribayashi. says.

616
00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:02.671
we're going to prepare these
defensive fortifications

617
00:32:02.671 --> 00:32:06.258
and wait. draw the Americans
in. and then attack them.

618
00:32:08.593 --> 00:32:10.887
- The Japanese
aren't on Iwo Jima;

619
00:32:10.887 --> 00:32:12.681
they're actually in Iwo Jima.

620
00:32:14.432 --> 00:32:16.560
They have miles and
miles of tunnels

621
00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:19.980
that they have dug out of
this soft volcanic terrain

622
00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:21.773
on the island.

623
00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:25.819
And they've also fortified
the most prominent landmark

624
00:32:25.819 --> 00:32:28.321
on Iwo Jima. which
is Mount Suribachi.

625
00:32:29.489 --> 00:32:32.284
- Looming over the
Marines' landing beaches.

626
00:32:32.284 --> 00:32:35.203
Mount Suribachi
provides the Japanese

627
00:32:35.203 --> 00:32:37.831
with a perfect
defensive position.

628
00:32:37.831 --> 00:32:40.417
- It's gonna be
this defensive hive

629
00:32:40.417 --> 00:32:44.462
that the Americans will
have to reduce cave by cave.

630
00:32:44.462 --> 00:32:47.549
bunker by bunker.
one thing at a time.

631
00:32:48.842 --> 00:32:52.512
- On Iwo Jima. Japanese
soldiers are issued orders

632
00:32:52.512 --> 00:32:54.931
to kill 10 Americans
before they die.

633
00:32:57.475 --> 00:32:59.144
- We see very few
Japanese

634
00:32:59.144 --> 00:33:00.604
who attempt to surrender.

635
00:33:00.604 --> 00:33:02.522
There's a view that this is a
great dishonor.

636
00:33:02.522 --> 00:33:05.275
not only to oneself
and one's family.

637
00:33:05.275 --> 00:33:06.943
but to the emperor as well.

638
00:33:09.196 --> 00:33:10.488
- If you look at
the letters

639
00:33:10.614 --> 00:33:12.616
that Kuribayashi writes
home to his family.

640
00:33:12.616 --> 00:33:14.451
he's clear right
from the beginning

641
00:33:14.451 --> 00:33:17.204
that no one on the Japanese
side is coming home

642
00:33:17.204 --> 00:33:18.747
from the Iwo Jima campaign.

643
00:33:18.747 --> 00:33:20.165
And he starts to
issue these orders

644
00:33:20.165 --> 00:33:23.460
very clearly to his men
saying. "Fight to the death."

645
00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:25.170
but he doesn't want
a banzai charge.

646
00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:28.215
He doesn't want
pointless suicidal acts.

647
00:33:28.215 --> 00:33:31.801
He wants to show the
Americans the cost to them

648
00:33:31.801 --> 00:33:33.970
of going as far as the
Japanese home islands.

649
00:33:43.021 --> 00:33:45.857
- The Navy bombarded
Japanese positions

650
00:33:45.857 --> 00:33:48.401
for three days.

651
00:33:49.861 --> 00:33:53.323
Now the Marines plan
to land 60.000 men

652
00:33:53.323 --> 00:33:56.201
across Iwo Jima's
southern beaches.

653
00:33:56.201 --> 00:33:58.995
push north. and take the island.

654
00:34:01.164 --> 00:34:04.042
- So at 8:59 on the
19th of February.

655
00:34:04.042 --> 00:34:08.255
the first 10.000 men
land. and it's quiet.

656
00:34:10.882 --> 00:34:13.551
But then at 10 o'clock
in the morning.

657
00:34:16.096 --> 00:34:18.681
every Japanese soldier
with a mortar. machine gun.

658
00:34:18.681 --> 00:34:19.975
or a piece of artillery

659
00:34:19.975 --> 00:34:22.686
will now start to shell
the landing beach.

660
00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:34.864
- In the face
of withering fire.

661
00:34:34.864 --> 00:34:38.368
the Marines struggle up
dunes of 15 feet or more.

662
00:34:41.288 --> 00:34:45.041
- Iwo Jima is not
sand; it's volcanic ash.

663
00:34:46.167 --> 00:34:47.793
Your feet just sink
up to your knees.

664
00:34:49.004 --> 00:34:50.964
It's like walking
in coffee grounds.

665
00:34:54.801 --> 00:34:57.137
It's incredibly deadly
to try and move forward.

666
00:34:59.848 --> 00:35:02.017
- Bulldozers are called
in to clear a path

667
00:35:02.017 --> 00:35:03.476
through the dunes.

668
00:35:03.476 --> 00:35:06.062
allowing the Marines to
continue their advance.

669
00:35:08.565 --> 00:35:10.358
- As the Marines
finally make their way

670
00:35:10.358 --> 00:35:12.402
off the beaches. they're getting
attacked from everywhere.

671
00:35:12.402 --> 00:35:13.903
people coming out of tunnels.

672
00:35:13.903 --> 00:35:15.739
of mortars coming off
of Mount Suribachi.

673
00:35:15.739 --> 00:35:18.408
of hidden artillery
emplacements all over the place

674
00:35:18.408 --> 00:35:21.077
with snipers firing positions
they can't even see.

675
00:35:22.203 --> 00:35:24.914
- Individual
soldier has to somehow

676
00:35:24.914 --> 00:35:27.083
get up close with a grenade

677
00:35:27.083 --> 00:35:29.252
that he can
put through the firing slit

678
00:35:29.252 --> 00:35:31.004
and kill the crew
that's in there.

679
00:35:33.548 --> 00:35:34.966
- Ultimately. one of
the greatest weapons

680
00:35:34.966 --> 00:35:38.762
that the Marines have is
what they'd call Zippo tanks.

681
00:35:40.388 --> 00:35:42.515
which are tank-sized
flamethrowers

682
00:35:43.808 --> 00:35:45.810
that can shoot long distances.

683
00:35:47.562 --> 00:35:49.230
and that becomes
the weapon of choice

684
00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:50.523
for Marines on Iwo Jima.

685
00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:01.242
- By the end
of the first day.

686
00:36:01.242 --> 00:36:03.286
the Marines have
secured an airfield

687
00:36:04.287 --> 00:36:06.790
and prepared to attack
Mount Suribachi.

688
00:36:08.375 --> 00:36:12.087
- On Iwo Jima. two
strategies come into conflict.

689
00:36:12.087 --> 00:36:14.798
One says. no surrender.
that's the Japanese.

690
00:36:14.798 --> 00:36:16.549
and then unconditional
surrender.

691
00:36:16.549 --> 00:36:18.843
and that's the
American position.

692
00:36:18.843 --> 00:36:20.470
And on Suribachi.

693
00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:23.139
the Americans make a
determined statement

694
00:36:23.139 --> 00:36:25.600
that it's going to be
unconditional surrender.

695
00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:27.852
There is no position
on planet Earth

696
00:36:27.852 --> 00:36:29.521
held by the Japanese army

697
00:36:30.605 --> 00:36:32.565
that American
troops cannot take.

698
00:36:42.283 --> 00:36:44.869
- After
securing the beaches.

699
00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:48.748
U.S. Marines on Iwo Jima
attack Mount Suribachi.

700
00:36:50.375 --> 00:36:51.626
- The force that
goes up the hill

701
00:36:51.626 --> 00:36:53.670
fights off Japanese resistance.

702
00:36:56.297 --> 00:36:58.883
and upon reaching the summit.

703
00:36:58.883 --> 00:37:00.301
they find a water pipe

704
00:37:00.301 --> 00:37:02.804
that had been associated
with a Japanese cistern.

705
00:37:02.804 --> 00:37:06.099
They attach a small American
flag to that water pipe.

706
00:37:06.099 --> 00:37:08.685
and then they raise that flag.

707
00:37:12.689 --> 00:37:15.024
- But they realize
the flag's too small.

708
00:37:15.024 --> 00:37:16.151
We need a bigger flag.

709
00:37:17.402 --> 00:37:18.737
So what they're gonna do

710
00:37:18.737 --> 00:37:20.280
is they're gonna
take a ship's flag

711
00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:21.322
and they're gonna
send it up there.

712
00:37:21.322 --> 00:37:23.366
And about noon.
it gets up there.

713
00:37:24.325 --> 00:37:27.954
- Six Marines plant
the second American flag.

714
00:37:27.954 --> 00:37:31.750
Joe Rosenthal. an Associated
Press photographer.

715
00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:33.084
captures the moment.

716
00:37:35.003 --> 00:37:38.423
- All the ships offshore
begin blowing their horns.

717
00:37:41.509 --> 00:37:43.094
Everyone on the
island can see it.

718
00:37:50.852 --> 00:37:53.229
- It's a snapshot in
time.

719
00:37:53.229 --> 00:37:55.148
The composition's
nearly perfect.

720
00:37:55.148 --> 00:37:56.858
although he basically
shoots it off his hip

721
00:37:56.858 --> 00:37:59.736
without even using his
rangefinder as he did it.

722
00:37:59.736 --> 00:38:01.988
You can't see anybody's face.

723
00:38:01.988 --> 00:38:04.282
And so really it becomes
everybody's picture.

724
00:38:07.202 --> 00:38:10.121
- Two days later.
Rosenthal's photograph

725
00:38:10.121 --> 00:38:13.500
is on the front page of
newspapers across America.

726
00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:16.628
President Roosevelt
immediately understands

727
00:38:16.628 --> 00:38:19.714
how inspirational
this single image is.

728
00:38:20.882 --> 00:38:23.259
- FDR was always a
frustrated journalist.

729
00:38:23.259 --> 00:38:25.845
He had been the editor
of the Harvard newspaper.

730
00:38:25.845 --> 00:38:28.765
He understood visual imagery.

731
00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:32.560
- It's American
determination

732
00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:34.938
embodied in a single photograph.

733
00:38:36.147 --> 00:38:37.607
- They're all
working together.

734
00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:41.027
and so it tells
a national story.

735
00:38:41.027 --> 00:38:43.029
It's not just those
guys on top of the hill

736
00:38:43.029 --> 00:38:44.405
that raised the flag.

737
00:38:44.531 --> 00:38:47.075
It's a nation that put that
flag on top of the hill.

738
00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:53.832
- But it's striking that
the flag isn't fully planted.

739
00:38:53.832 --> 00:38:57.877
It's as if the struggle
is still unfolding.

740
00:38:57.877 --> 00:39:01.506
and that fit FDR's
mood at that point.

741
00:39:01.506 --> 00:39:03.132
This isn't over.

742
00:39:03.132 --> 00:39:04.843
We're getting closer.

743
00:39:04.843 --> 00:39:06.719
but we haven't gotten there yet.

744
00:39:06.719 --> 00:39:09.556
- To FDR.
these six Marines

745
00:39:09.556 --> 00:39:12.976
are the perfect symbol of
America's wartime effort.

746
00:39:12.976 --> 00:39:15.562
- He calls the Marines and
says. "Find me these guys.

747
00:39:15.562 --> 00:39:17.397
I want to bring them
to the Oval Office.

748
00:39:17.397 --> 00:39:19.649
I want to send them
on a war bond drive."

749
00:39:19.649 --> 00:39:22.569
- So the Marine Corps
sends somebody to the island.

750
00:39:22.569 --> 00:39:24.612
but they find that three
men who were in the photo

751
00:39:24.612 --> 00:39:26.239
were dead already.

752
00:39:26.239 --> 00:39:28.658
They died within days of
the photograph being taken.

753
00:39:31.536 --> 00:39:35.123
- U.S. Marines
suffered 26.000 dead.

754
00:39:35.123 --> 00:39:36.624
wounded. and missing.

755
00:39:38.167 --> 00:39:42.505
Almost every Japanese soldier
on Iwo Jima is killed.

756
00:39:44.632 --> 00:39:46.134
- Kuribayashi himself.

757
00:39:46.134 --> 00:39:48.595
who never believed he was
getting off Iwo Jima alive.

758
00:39:48.595 --> 00:39:51.264
he retreats. and he
takes his own life.

759
00:39:53.474 --> 00:39:54.809
- It's the only
battle in the Pacific

760
00:39:54.934 --> 00:39:56.811
where the Americans actually
take more casualties

761
00:39:56.811 --> 00:39:58.646
than the Japanese take.

762
00:39:58.646 --> 00:40:00.565
So for American
military planners.

763
00:40:00.565 --> 00:40:02.442
the lesson of Iwo Jima
seems pretty clear.

764
00:40:02.442 --> 00:40:04.444
that if you're going to
launch Operation Downfall.

765
00:40:04.444 --> 00:40:05.695
if you're actually
going to invade

766
00:40:05.695 --> 00:40:07.322
the Japanese home islands.

767
00:40:07.322 --> 00:40:09.282
the cost is going to be immense.

768
00:40:09.282 --> 00:40:11.117
because at this
point in the war.

769
00:40:11.117 --> 00:40:12.535
most Americans have concluded

770
00:40:12.535 --> 00:40:14.454
that Japan is never
going to surrender.

771
00:40:17.999 --> 00:40:20.251
- After
victory on Iwo Jima.

772
00:40:20.251 --> 00:40:23.254
the Marine Corps sends
three surviving flag raisers

773
00:40:23.254 --> 00:40:25.006
back to Washington.

774
00:40:25.006 --> 00:40:28.009
but they will not
meet their President.

775
00:40:31.512 --> 00:40:32.722
- Ladies
and gentlemen.

776
00:40:32.722 --> 00:40:34.641
the tragic news of
the President's death

777
00:40:34.641 --> 00:40:37.560
came to Washington today
as a complete shock.

778
00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:40.521
except to a very few people
who knew of his condition.

779
00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:46.110
- Franklin Roosevelt had

780
00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:49.197
loomed so large for so long.

781
00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:51.824
An entire generation
of Americans

782
00:40:51.824 --> 00:40:54.535
really only knew him
as their President.

783
00:40:55.578 --> 00:40:58.039
Supposedly during the
funeral procession.

784
00:40:58.039 --> 00:41:01.668
a man is so overcome
by his emotions

785
00:41:01.668 --> 00:41:05.672
and another man says. "Did
you know the President?"

786
00:41:05.672 --> 00:41:09.801
And he says. "No.
but he knew me."

787
00:41:09.801 --> 00:41:12.512
and that's how people felt
about Franklin Roosevelt.

788
00:41:14.263 --> 00:41:17.100
- Roosevelt had been a
very. very active President.

789
00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:19.978
a very. very
powerful personality.

790
00:41:19.978 --> 00:41:22.522
the kind of caretaker
to the people.

791
00:41:22.522 --> 00:41:25.858
and he knew you can't
fight an endless war

792
00:41:25.858 --> 00:41:28.277
if you want to maintain
your democratic ideals.

793
00:41:29.696 --> 00:41:34.200
He also knew how difficult
unconditional surrender can be

794
00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:36.077
to put into actual practice.

795
00:41:37.954 --> 00:41:40.248
- During years of
fear and uncertainty.

796
00:41:40.248 --> 00:41:42.083
Roosevelt was uniquely able

797
00:41:42.083 --> 00:41:45.962
to lead America's colossal
fight against fascism.

798
00:41:45.962 --> 00:41:48.881
Now. this job will fall
to his vice President.

799
00:41:48.881 --> 00:41:49.799
Harry Truman.

800
00:41:53.219 --> 00:41:57.557
- Mrs. Roosevelt summons
Truman to the White House.

801
00:41:57.557 --> 00:42:01.352
He says. "Is there anything
we can do for you?"

802
00:42:01.352 --> 00:42:02.729
And Mrs. Roosevelt says.

803
00:42:02.729 --> 00:42:05.481
"No. Harry. is there
anything we can do for you?

804
00:42:06.524 --> 00:42:08.526
For you are the one
in trouble now."

805
00:42:11.738 --> 00:42:15.908
- On assuming the presidency.
Truman told reporters.

806
00:42:15.908 --> 00:42:17.827
"I felt like the
moon. the stars.

807
00:42:17.827 --> 00:42:21.080
and all the planets
had fallen on me."

808
00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:24.625
Harry Truman of Missouri
is now responsible

809
00:42:24.625 --> 00:42:27.754
for the successful conclusion
of the largest conflict

810
00:42:27.754 --> 00:42:29.464
in human history.

811
00:42:29.464 --> 00:42:31.674
including the final destruction

812
00:42:31.674 --> 00:42:34.010
of Adolf Hitler's Third Reich.

812
